{
  "id": "middle-of-the-linked-list",
  "questionId": "middle-of-the-linked-list",
  "slug": "middle-of-the-linked-list",
  "title": "Middle of the Linked List",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "linked list"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you could count the length first and walk there, but is there a way to find the middle in a single pass without knowing the length up front?",
    "what if one pointer moved twice as fast as another? when the fast one finishes, where would the slow one be?",
    "start both pointers at the head; each step, move slow by one node and fast by two nodes",
    "when fast reaches the end of the list (or has no next node to jump to), slow is sitting at the middle - return from slow onward"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "off-by-one on the even-length case, returning the first middle instead of the second",
    "not checking for fast being null before accessing fast.next when advancing two steps",
    "counting the length first when a single-pass two-pointer approach is expected"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]`\nis the value of the i-th node and the nodes are connected in order (node\n`i` points to node `i+1`).\n\n**Return the second half of the list, starting at the middle node.**\n\nIf the list has an even number of nodes, treat the middle as the second\nof the two center nodes (index `n/2` using integer division), so the\nsecond half is the longer or equal half.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 100",
      "-1000 <= nums[i] <= 1000"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node list",
      "a two-node list",
      "an odd-length list vs an even-length list"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
